test: cover the two pieces of code the suite never executed

Measured rather than guessed: a coverage run puts the suite at 96.1% with no
admin controller below 90%, and the authorization matrix the audit recorded as a
gap turns out to be covered by fifteen org-scope guard files plus the API rights
matrix — what the audit lacked was live probing, not tests. Writing tests broadly
would have been activity, not coverage.

Two things genuinely had none.

users:enforce-email-uniqueness is the operator's remedy for the one state the
email-uniqueness migration may leave behind: when case-variant duplicates already
exist it installs a NON-unique index rather than failing the deploy, and this
command upgrades it once they are resolved. OidcUserResolver matches on
lower(email) while the column's constraint is case-sensitive, so a surviving pair
is an identity-confusion state the resolver has to pick between — and the tool
for repairing it had never been proven to work. Covers the healthy path, the
refusal that names each colliding address and account count, the upgrade after
the operator resolves it, and idempotence.

SweepOciStorage deletes blobs, manifests and upload sessions; only its dispatch
was asserted, so handle() never ran and nothing proved the one thing the job adds
over calling the sweeper directly — forwarding the configured blob budget.

That test surfaced a latent trap, pinned as current behaviour and explicitly
marked as is-not-should: config/kontorfix.php reads
`(int) env('KONTORFIX_OCI_SWEEP_BLOB_LIMIT', 1000)`, so an env var that is
present but empty casts to 0, and the 1000 fallback inside the job is dead code
because the config key always exists. A scheduled sweep then removes nothing,
permanently, with no error while storage grows. Hardening it is a behaviour
change and is left as a decision.

One test asserts against captured Artisan output instead of chained
expectsOutputToContain(): the command does print "root@firma.de (2 Konten)", but
the chained matcher does not see the second substring on the same line.

